<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<FindBugsFilter xmlns="https://github.com/spotbugs/filter/3.0.0">

    <!--
      EI_EXPOSE_REP2 fires when a constructor stores a reference to an
      externally mutable object. That is a real defect for value objects
      holding arrays or Dates, and the detector stays enabled for those.

      It is a false positive for Spring constructor injection: storing the
      injected collaborator in a final field is the entire mechanism. The
      alternative the detector implies - defensively copying an injected
      singleton - is not possible and would be wrong if it were.

      Scoped to constructors of the Spring bean layers only, so entities and
      value objects elsewhere are still checked.
    -->
    <Match>
        <Class name="~com\.quizforge\..*\.(app|web|security|idempotency)\..*"/>
        <Method name="&lt;init&gt;"/>
        <Or>
            <Bug pattern="EI_EXPOSE_REP"/>
            <Bug pattern="EI_EXPOSE_REP2"/>
        </Or>
    </Match>

    <!--
      Payload records defensively copy their lists into an unmodifiable
      wrapper in a compact constructor, so the accessor cannot leak anything
      mutable. SpotBugs cannot see through Collections.unmodifiableList and
      reports the accessor regardless.

      This suppression is justified by a test, not by assertion:
      QuestionPayloadTest.payloadsAreImmutableAfterConstruction and
      ImportReportTest.isImmutableAfterConstruction each mutate the list passed
      to the constructor, assert the value is unchanged, then assert the
      accessor's list rejects modification. If that property is ever lost, a
      test fails - the suppression cannot hide it.
    -->
    <Match>
        <Class name="~com\.quizforge\.content\.(domain\.payload|importer)\..*"/>
        <Or>
            <Bug pattern="EI_EXPOSE_REP"/>
            <Bug pattern="EI_EXPOSE_REP2"/>
        </Or>
    </Match>

    <!--
      PREDICTABLE_RANDOM on the seeded shuffle.

      Determinism is the requirement, not an oversight: an attempt must render
      the same question order every time it is asked, so a refresh mid-question
      does not reshuffle the options under the player. SecureRandom cannot do
      that - it has no reproducible seed - so the detector is objecting to the
      only mechanism that satisfies the requirement.

      The seed is derived from the attempt's UUIDv7 by XORing its halves. The
      timestamp prefix is guessable, but the remaining 74 random bits are not,
      so predicting another player's shuffle is not feasible. Nothing secret
      depends on it in any case: the shuffle hides no answer, it only varies
      presentation between players.

      Proven by AttemptServiceTest.anAttemptRendersTheSameOrderEveryTimeItIsAsked.
    -->
    <Match>
        <Or>
            <Class name="com.quizforge.play.app.AttemptService"/>
            <Class name="com.quizforge.content.app.QuestionAccessAdapter"/>
        </Or>
        <Bug pattern="PREDICTABLE_RANDOM"/>
    </Match>

    <!--
      PageWindow.Slice copies its list into an unmodifiable wrapper in a compact
      constructor, so the accessor cannot leak anything mutable. SpotBugs cannot
      see through List.copyOf and reports the accessor regardless - the same
      false positive as the payload records above.

      Justified by a test rather than by assertion:
      PageWindowTest.aSliceCannotBeMutatedAfterConstruction mutates the list
      passed in, asserts the slice is unchanged, then asserts the returned list
      rejects modification. If that property is ever lost, a test fails.
    -->
    <Match>
        <Class name="~com\.quizforge\.platform\.web\..*"/>
        <Or>
            <Bug pattern="EI_EXPOSE_REP"/>
            <Bug pattern="EI_EXPOSE_REP2"/>
        </Or>
    </Match>

    <!--
      Everything under com.quizforge.api is generated from openapi.yaml by
      openapi-generator at build time. It is not in version control, nobody
      wrote it, and any edit would be discarded by the next build.

      This is an exclusion of generated code, not a suppression of a finding.
      The models do expose mutable lists - EI_EXPOSE_REP is technically correct
      about them - but the only way to act on it would be to stop generating
      them. Reporting a defect nobody can fix trains readers to skim the report,
      which is the failure ADR 0007 recorded for the legacy package.

      The hand-written code that builds these models is still analysed, so a
      leak introduced by a controller is still caught.
    -->
    <Match>
        <Class name="~com\.quizforge\.api\..*"/>
    </Match>

</FindBugsFilter>
